 5. 🏭 Climate&#x27;s SCOTUS showdown 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The Supreme Court on Monday will hear arguments in a case that could determine whether states and cities can seek climate damages from oil giants.

 Why it matters: Billions and billions of dollars are at stake for the oil industry in dozens of cases brought by state and local governments, in which they seek to hold the companies responsible for the mounting costs of climate change, Axios' Ben Geman writes. ⚖️ The big picture: SCOTUS is considering whether federal law blocks state-law damage claims for the effects of interstate and global emissions, in a case against Exxon and Suncor that the city and county of Boulder filed in 2018.

 But dozens of state-court cases are active in California, Connecticut, New York, Massachusetts and beyond: They accuse oil giants of concealing risks or making deceptive statements, and seek damages for climate-related costs. What we're watching: This is a blockbuster. The decision will be the culmination of an intense, yearslong battle.

 There's a high level of organizing, lobbying and advocacy among industry and climate advocates alike around these state cases — a sign that both sides see litigation as a critical battleground on climate. Read on . 

 
 
